    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d366">

                <pb facs="0375" n="293" sameAs="frus:frus1911/pg293"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 815.00/929.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The American Minister to Guatemala to the Secretary of
                        State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1910-12-21"><hi rendition="#italic">December 21,
                        1910</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Hitt says he is in receipt of a telegram from the consular agent at
                    Livingston which states that rifles and ammunition were placed on board the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Centenalla</hi> on the 16th, and that she proceeded to
                    Manambique Point, where it is supposed she still was on the 19th; that the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Emma</hi> left Livingston, proceeding in the direction
                    of Gra-ciosa Bay, with arms; that it is reported that 45 Hondurans left for
                    Graciosa Bay; that 25 Honduran strangers who arrived in Livingston on the 19th
                    are expected to leave at any moment; that it is reported that the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Hornet</hi> is expected, and that the Guatemalan
                    minister for foreign affairs promises adequate measures. Mr. Hitt has
                    telegraphed the above information to the American minister to Hopduras.</p>
            </div>
